New opt-in option `--lemma-bindings` (requires `--type-system-refresh`). With it, an expression `var y1, ..., yk := L(args); E`, where `L` is a lemma with `k` out-parameters, binds the variables, which are ghost, to results of the call: values that have the declared types and satisfy `L`'s postcondition. The call is checked as the call statement is (precondition, termination, and under `--modular-termination` the same classification), and a function that binds results of a lemma whose postcondition reads the heap must be allowed to read what the postcondition reads. The results are chosen as a function of the lemma, its type arguments, receiver and in-arguments (and the heap, where the postcondition depends on it), so the same call binds the same results everywhere; they are not the values the lemma's body computes, and the results of a call statement need not equal them. Only an ordinary lemma can be bound, with result types that satisfy `(!new)` and a postcondition that a function body could contain (no `old`, `fresh`, `unchanged`, `allocated`, two-state function or quantification over the allocated references); a heap-dependent postcondition may not bind results of its own lemma. A bound result can be a trigger, written `var y := L(args); y`; a larger term that contains a binding is not selected as one. A library that binds results of a lemma can be used only by a client that sets the option. Without the option, programs get the errors and output they got before, and the error for such a let gains a hint under `--show-hints` (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/199).
